    "passage": "THE STEP PYRAMID OF DJOSER: BIRTH OF EGYPTIAN MONUMENTAL ARCHITECTURE\n\nParagraph A: In the 27th century BC, Pharaoh Djoser reigned over the Third Dynasty of ancient Egypt. Prior to his ascension, Egyptian royal tombs were exclusively mastabas—rectangular, flat-roofed mud-brick structures. Djoser, however, conceived a burial monument that would mirror his divine status. He commissioned his brilliant vizier and royal architect, Imhotep.\n\nParagraph B: Imhotep envisioned a radically unprecedented structural design at Saqqara, overlooking Memphis. Instead of a single flat mastaba, he stacked six progressively smaller mastabas atop one another, creating the world's first true step pyramid, rising over 62 meters (204 feet) into the desert sky.\n\nParagraph C: Unlike preceding structures built from sun-baked mud bricks, the Step Pyramid was constructed entirely of carved limestone blocks. This revolution in materials required sophisticated logistics, masonry standardization, and a massive organized workforce, demonstrating the immense centralized power of the Third Dynasty state.\n\nParagraph D: Beneath the pyramid lay a subterranean labyrinth of tunnels extending for over six kilometers. At its center was the pharaoh's burial chamber, hewn from solid bedrock and encased in massive slabs of pink Aswan granite, accessible only through a vertical shaft sealed with a 3.5-tonne stone plug.",
