    "passage": "THE DEVELOPMENT OF THE LONDON UNDERGROUND RAILWAY\n\nParagraph A: In the first half of the 19th century, London's population expanded explosively from one million to over two and a half million. The city's narrow medieval thoroughfares were paralyzed by an estimated 200,000 daily commuters navigating horse-drawn omnibuses, cabs, and private carriages. Overcrowding, pollution, and gridlock threatened the commercial vibrancy of the world's greatest metropolis.\n\nParagraph B: In the 1840s, Charles Pearson, the City of London Solicitor, proposed a revolutionary solution: a subterranean railway linking the suburban railway termini of Euston, King's Cross, and Paddington with the financial heart of the City at Farringdon. Critics derided the scheme as dangerous madness, fearing tunnels would collapse under the weight of London traffic.\n\nParagraph C: Construction on the Metropolitan Railway began in 1860 using the 'cut-and-cover' technique. A massive trench was excavated along existing roadways, brick arch walls were constructed, and the street was reconstructed above. On 10 January 1863, the line opened to public acclaim, transporting nearly 40,000 passengers on its first operational day.\n\nParagraph D: Although the early line used steam locomotives that filled stations with soot and sulfurous fumes, its commercial triumph was absolute. In 1890, the City and South London Railway introduced electric traction and deep-level circular tunnels excavated by the Greathead shield, inaugurating the world's modern underground tube network.",
